Yes. Pantheon systems first, external program archetypes second.

The Maestro’s routing order should be:

\boxed{
\text{Objective}
\rightarrow
\text{Pantheon-native operators}
\rightarrow
\text{existing compositions}
\rightarrow
\text{external archetype if needed}
\rightarrow
\text{new hybrid}
}

That matters because otherwise we accidentally rebuild Pantheon around familiar software metaphors instead of using the machinery we already developed.

So if the problem is “I know the outcome I want but not how to reach it,” the first thought should not automatically be Excel → Goal Seek. It should be:

Do we already have Semantic Goal Seek, Rangefinder, Scout Doctrine, Adaptive Navigation, Metamap, Indigo Hammer, ZPC/Kintsugi, Interface Forge, PatternDex, Narrative Operators, etc. that fit this problem?

Then Excel’s Goal Seek can be mined comparatively:

\text{Pantheon Goal Seek}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Excel Goal Seek}

Ask:

* What does Excel know that ours doesn’t?
* What does ours know that Excel doesn’t?
* What invariant do both instantiate?
* Can their differences produce a stronger operator?

That gives us comparative distillation, not replacement.

The Maestro should therefore have roughly three shelves:

Pantheon Instruments — native systems and operators we deliberately developed.

Natural Instruments — mechanisms mined from biology, physics, cognition, narrative, social systems, etc.

Program Instruments — computational patterns mined from Excel, Git, CAD, IDEs, game engines, DAWs, databases, compilers, and so on.

Then the Maestro selects across all three, but with an important priority:

\boxed{\text{Check the Pantheon before importing another god.}}

Not because Pantheon must always win. The opposite: because we should know what we already possess before duplicating it.

And if an outside system is better, steal the good part.

For example:

\text{Git branching}
+
\text{Pantheon Games-Within-Games}
+
\text{Semantic Diff}

could become something better than “Semantic Git.”

Likewise:

\text{Excel Goal Seek}
+
\text{Semantic Goal Seek}
+
\text{Metamap}
+
\text{ACT}

becomes:

Specify desired state → identify movable variables and semantic variables → trace dependencies → search interventions → simulate consequences → choose next actionable move → update from reality.

That’s much more Pantheon.

So yes: Pantheon first. Then raid the world for upgrades.